1.
First clarify the requirements
Before writing any class, ask questions.

For a vending machine :-

Products
 What types of products can it sell?
 Does every product have a different price?
 Can multiple products have the same type?
 Do products have an expiry date?

Inventory
    How many products can each slot hold?
    What happens when a product is out of stock?
    Can an administrator refill the machine?

Payment
    What payment methods are supported? :- Cash? Card? UPI?
    Can the user insert money in multiple steps?
    Can the machine return change?
    What happens if insufficient money is inserted?

Transaction
     Can the user cancel?
     Should inserted money be refunded?
     What happens if the machine runs out of change?
     What happens if dispensing fails?

Admin
    Do we need an admin interface?
    Can an admin add/remove products?
    Can an admin collect money?

For an interview, you could say :-

I'll assume the machine supports multiple products, cash payment, inventory management, 
cancellation, and change return.
I'll keep card/UPI and admin operations extensible but won't implement them unless required.

2
Identify the main entities
Now extract the nouns from the requirements.

VendingMachine
Product
Slot
Inventory
Payment
Transaction

What responsibility belongs to each?

For example :-
   Product :- id , name , price
   Slot :- slotId , product , quantity
   Inventory :- Collections of slots
   VendingMachine :-  Inventory , PaymentProcessor , PaymentProcessor

3
Identify the machine's states (if applicable)
This is very important for Vending Machine.

Think about what the machine is doing.
IDLE -> PRODUCT_SELECTED -> PAYMENT_PENDING -> DISPENSING -> IDLE

And there can be :-
PAYMENT_PENDING ->  CANCEL -> REFUND -> IDLE

The State Pattern might be useful.

The machine's behavior changes significantly depending on its current state, so I'll model 
the states explicitly.

4
Identify Responsibilities

Don't make one giant class:
  Instead think :-
      VendingMachine :- Inventory , PaymentProcessor , ProductDispenser , State

Inventory
   Responsible for :- 
         isAvailable() , getProduct() , removeProduct() , addProduct()

PaymentProcessor
    Responsible for :-
          acceptPayment() , calculateChange() , refund()

Dispenser
    Responsible for :- dispenseProduct()

VendingMachine
     Coordinates everything. That's separation of responsibility.

Data Structure can be decided like , 

(i)
Suppose the machine has slots : A1 → Coke → 5 , A2 → Pepsi → 3 , B1 → Chips → 7
Map<String, Slot> slots;

(ii)
For change-making, you might maintain : Map<Coin, Integer> coins;

Decide where interfaces are useful :-

Suppose initially you only support cash.
You could do :-
class CashPaymentProcessor {
    ...
}

But tomorrow the interviewer says :- "Add card payment."
You don't want to heavily modify your vending machine.

interface PaymentProcessor {
    PaymentResult processPayment(Money amount);
    void refund();
}

Give a real life example :-
"Walk me through what happens when I buy a Coke."

User selects Coke -> Machine checks inventory -> Machine checks price -> Machine accepts payment
-> Payment successful? -> Calculate change -> Dispense Coke -> Update inventory -> Return change
-> Transaction complete


Think about failure cases

Ask :- 
(i)
Product unavailable
User selects Coke -> No Coke -> Reject transaction

(ii)
Insufficient money
Price = ₹50
Inserted = ₹30 → Don't dispense → Keep transaction pending

(iii) 
User cancels
₹50 inserted -> Cancel -> Refund ₹50 -> IDLE

(iv)
Machine doesn't have enough change
Suppose :- Product = ₹30 , User inserts = ₹100
If the machine can't return ₹70 → Don't dispense → Refund ₹100

